1.2.1.8 Diagrams
................

‘diagram=true’ sets up a figure that is a picture rather than a graph:
axes, grid, legend and hover off, equal x and y scales
(‘aspect_ratio=true’) and ‘height=fit’.  Options given explicitly win,
e.g. ‘diagram=true, xaxis=true’.

‘height=fit’ computes the height at which the plot area shows ‘xrange’
and ‘yrange’ at the aspect ratio (1, or ‘aspect_ratio’ if it is a
number) with no space left over, for the ‘width’ (700 by default) and
margins.  It needs both ranges.  Without a ‘margin’ option it assumes
Aximar's fixed margins (left 50, right 30, top 40, bottom 50 pixels) and
sets them explicitly, so other hosts lay the plot out the same way.
With ‘diagram=true’ and automatic ranges, the height falls back to the
default square plot.

‘theme="dark"’ uses dark backgrounds, light text and grid colours suited
to a dark host, and makes uncoloured ‘vector’ arrows and shape outlines
light too.  ‘plot_bgcolor’ / ‘paper_bgcolor’ still override the
background.  Aximar makes plot backgrounds transparent, so there the
theme mostly sets the text and line colours.

ax_draw2d(
  polygon([[0,0], [1,1], [2,0], [1,-1]], fill_color="rgba(150,150,230,0.3)"),
  label(["I", 1, 0], font_size=20),
  diagram=true, theme="dark", xrange=[-0.2, 2.2], yrange=[-1.2, 1.2]
)$


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Shapes,  Next: Raw Plotly,  Prev: Diagrams,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.9 Shapes
..............

‘rectangle’, ‘ellipse’, ‘hline’ and ‘vline’ are drawn as Plotly layout
shapes: they never appear in the legend or take hover.  ‘rectangle’ and
‘ellipse’ take the draw package's arguments.  The outline uses ‘color’,
‘line_width’ and ‘dash’; ‘rectangle’ and ‘ellipse’ are filled only when
‘fill_color’ is set.  ‘layer="below"’ draws a shape underneath the data.
Like ‘label’, shapes are not yet supported inside ‘animate(...)’.

ax_draw2d(
  rectangle([0, 0], [1, 1], fill_color="rgba(0,150,0,0.15)", layer="below"),
  ellipse(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.25, color="blue"),
  ellipse(0, 0, 0.3, 0.3, 0, 90, fill_color="orange"),
  hline(0.5, dash="dot"), vline(0.5, dash="dot"),
  explicit(x^2, x, 0, 1),
  aspect_ratio=true
)$


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Raw Plotly,  Next: Data Values,  Prev: Shapes,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.10 Raw Plotly
...................

Anything not wrapped by ax-plots can be passed through as JSON:
‘ax_trace("{...}")’ adds a Plotly trace verbatim, and
‘layout_raw="{...}"’ appends keys to the layout, overriding ax-plots'
own values (annotations from ‘label’ / ‘vector’ still take precedence).

ax_draw2d(
  explicit(x^2, x, -1, 1),
  ax_trace("{\"type\":\"bar\",\"x\":[-0.5,0.5],\"y\":[0.25,0.25],\"opacity\":0.4}"),
  layout_raw="{\"hovermode\":\"x unified\"}"
)$


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Data Values,  Next: Per-object Options,  Prev: Raw Plotly,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.11 Data Values
....................

‘lines’ and ‘points’ data may contain exact numeric constants such as
‘%pi/2’, ‘sqrt(2)’ or ‘atan(3)’; they are converted with ‘float’.  A
value that is still not a number (for example a free symbol ‘a’) is an
error naming the object, axis and index.  Use ‘false’ (or ‘inf’, ‘minf’,
‘und’, ‘ind’) for an intentional gap in a line.  Numeric constants are
also accepted in ‘xrange’ / ‘yrange’, e.g. ‘xrange=[-%pi, %pi]’.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Per-object Options,  Next: Lists and Groups,  Prev: Data Values,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.12 Per-object Options
...........................

Style options can be given as trailing arguments inside an object.  They
apply to that object only and do not change the options in effect for
later objects:

ax_draw2d(
  color="blue",
  lines([0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 0], color="red", dash="dot", showlegend=false),
  lines([0, 1, 2], [1, 0, 1])          /* still blue */
)$

‘showlegend’ inside an object hides (or shows) that trace's legend
entry; at the top level ‘showlegend’ is the layout option for the whole
legend.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Lists and Groups,  Next: ,  Prev: Per-object Options,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.13 Lists and Groups
.........................

Any argument may be a list of objects (and options); lists are spliced
in place, at any depth, so a figure built in a loop can be passed
directly:

objs: makelist(lines([0, 1], [0, k]), k, 1, 5)$
ax_draw2d(objs, title="Fan", aspect_ratio=true)$

‘group(obj, ..., opt=val, ...)’ gives every object in it the group's
options, as if each were written inside the object.  The options apply
to the group only, and an object's own options still win.  Groups may
contain lists and other groups:

ax_draw2d(
  group(objs, color="gray", line_width=1, hoverinfo="skip"),
  group(lines([0, 1], [0, 2.5], color="red"), lines([0, 1], [0, 5.5]), dash="dot"),
  title="Fan"
)$

Layout options such as ‘title’ can't go in a group.  ‘ax_draw3d’ accepts
lists and groups the same way.

